<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">Li County, Gansu</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Lixian</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Li County, or Lixian, is a county in Longnan in south-eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gansu">Gansu</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a>, in the upper valley of the Western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/han-river">Han River</a>. Excavations there, including large tombs at Dabuzishan attributed to early <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qin-dynasty">Qin</a> rulers, show that a Qin elite was established in the area by the eighth century BCE. It is regarded as the early <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bantustan">homeland</a> of the Qin before they moved east into the Wei valley.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/li-county-gansu.jpg" alt="Li County, Gansu" width="320" height="254" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Croquant · CC BY 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Location_of_Longnan_Prefecture_within_Gansu_(China).png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
